//! lotus/sidechain.rye — ALES63: the sidechain compressor — the gain that follows ANOTHER signal. The held dynamics quartet
//! (ALES57–62) is whole: gate, compressor, limiter, and expander each drive their gain from ALES56's one proven held follower. Yet
//! every one of them reads its detector from the SAME signal it processes — the compressor listens to the very track it turns down. A
//! real console does one more thing that opens a whole wing of the craft: it lets the detector listen to a DIFFERENT signal. A kick
//! keys a compressor on the bass so the bass ducks each time the kick lands; a voice keys a compressor on a music bed so the music
//! steps back whenever someone speaks; the sibilant band of a vocal keys a compressor on the vocal itself so only the "ess" is tamed.
//! All of these are one primitive — a SIDECHAIN, a detector that follows a KEY signal while the gain lands on a TARGET signal. This
//! rung is that primitive, and the crux that opens the wing: once a detector reads a separate key, ducking, de-essing, and keyed
//! gating all follow as compositions rather than new machinery. It re-runs ALES58's exact per-sample loop with one change — the held
//! follower advances on the KEY's sample rather than the target's own, and the resulting gain lands on the target. The gain math is
//! ALES50's softened ceiling, not re-invented; the time base is ALES56's held env_step, not re-invented; a self-key reduces to ALES58
//! byte-for-byte, so the sidechain GENERALIZES the proven in-line compressor rather than replacing it.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES63.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260814-184728_fill-ales63-lotus-sidechain-compressor.md
//! Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, sample_min/sample_max, max_clip, the range law shape; lotus/compress.rye
//! (ALES50) — the softened ceiling this drives, re-proven beside it; lotus/hold_env.rye (ALES56) — the HELD time base, its
//! env_step_hold advancing the KEY's follower one sample at a time with the hold pinning the peak; lotus/compress_env_hold.rye
//! (ALES58) — the attack/hold/release compressor this keys, reduced to byte-for-byte when the key holds the target's own samples.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM buffers on one bench, siloed to lotus/. No socket,
//! no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate — the attack, release, and hold are counted in sample INDICES,
//! not milliseconds against a clock; the threshold is a magnitude in sample units, the ratio a plain fraction; there is no lookahead
//! and no external key routing. One held envelope step and at most one multiply-divide per sample; nothing on the path can overflow,
//! since the compressed envelope is at most the envelope so the gain sits in (0, 1] and every written magnitude is at most the target
//! input's, which already fits i16.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/sidechain.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const envelope = @import("envelope.rye");
const hold_env = @import("hold_env.rye");
const compress_env_hold = @import("compress_env_hold.rye");

pub const sidechain_version = "20260814.184728";

/// The sidechain compressor's faults, one consistent name each: a threshold outside [1, sample_max], a ratio below unity or with a
/// zero denominator, an attack or release coefficient outside (0, 1], a hold longer than the widest clip, a span outside the TARGET
/// samples, and a KEY clip too short to cover the span. The first five coincide by name with ALES58's — BadThreshold and BadRatio from
/// the compressor, BadCoeff from the follower, BadHold from the held follower, BadRange from the whole family — so a composer sees one
/// consistent fault surface; BadKey is the one fault a keyed detector adds, named for the second clip it reads.
pub const SidechainError = error{
    /// A threshold of zero (no band left to compress below it) or above sample_max (nothing could ever cross it).
    BadThreshold,
    /// A ratio below unity (would expand, not compress) or with a zero denominator — refused, no write.
    BadRatio,
    /// An attack or release coefficient outside (0, 1] — a frozen envelope, an overshooting step, or a divide by zero.
    BadCoeff,
    /// A hold longer than max_clip samples — no clip is that long, so such a hold could never expire within a span; refused, no write.
    BadHold,
    /// A span outside the current TARGET samples — refused, no write.
    BadRange,
    /// A KEY clip shorter than the span — the detector would read past the key's samples; refused, no write.
    BadKey,
};

/// Compress `count` samples starting at `start` in place in `target`, the gain reduction FOLLOWING a HELD envelope driven by the `key`
/// clip's samples over the SAME span rather than by the target's own — the sidechain: the detector listens to the key while the gain
/// lands on the target. The held envelope CONTINUES from the caller's `env` and `hold_left` (ALES43's carried-state lesson, carrying
/// both the envelope and the hold countdown so the key's detector survives a call boundary). Each sample advances the proven ALES56
/// held follower one step on `key.buf[start + i]`; when the held KEY envelope sits within the threshold the target sample passes
/// byte-for-byte, and when it rises above, the envelope's excess is divided by the ratio (ALES50's softened ceiling) and that gain
/// applied to the raw TARGET sample. When `key` holds the same samples as `target` and both are read unmutated, this reduces to
/// ALES58's compress_hold_follow_carry byte-for-byte (ALES58 reads each target sample before it writes, so an identical key reproduces
/// its detector). Refuses BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadCoeff, BadHold, BadRange, and BadKey before any write; on refusal the target and
/// the carried state are untouched. Target values only — never a length, and never the key.
pub fn compress_key_follow_carry(
    target: *timeline.Clip,
    key: *const timeline.Clip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    env: *i64,
    hold_left: *u32,
    threshold: i32,
    ratio_num: u32,
    ratio_den: u32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
    hold_samples: u32,
) SidechainError!void {
    // invariant: the threshold is a legal magnitude — at least one sample unit and no greater than the i16 rail. Before any write.
    if (threshold < 1) return error.BadThreshold;
    if (threshold > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadThreshold;
    // invariant: the ratio is at least one (num >= den) and its denominator is nonzero — a ratio below one would expand.
    if (ratio_den == 0) return error.BadRatio;
    if (ratio_num < ratio_den) return error.BadRatio;
    // invariant: both attack and release coefficients are legal fractions in (0, 1] — checked by the follower's one proven rule.
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(attack_num, attack_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(release_num, release_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    // invariant: the hold is bounded by the widest clip — a longer hold could never expire within any span. Before any write.
    if (hold_samples > timeline.max_clip) return error.BadHold;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current TARGET samples — checked without underflow (count vs the remainder).
    if (start > target.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > target.len - start) return error.BadRange;
    // invariant: the KEY covers the whole span too — the detector never reads past the key's samples. Checked without underflow.
    if (start > key.len) return error.BadKey;
    if (count > key.len - start) return error.BadKey;

    const thr: i64 = @as(i64, threshold);
    const num: i64 = @as(i64, ratio_num);
    const den: i64 = @as(i64, ratio_den);
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < count) : (i += 1) {
        const k: i16 = key.buf[start + i]; // the KEY sample drives the detector
        const x: i16 = target.buf[start + i]; // the TARGET sample receives the gain
        // Advance the proven ALES56 HELD follower one sample ON THE KEY; e is the smoothed-and-held key magnitude, in [0, sample_max].
        _ = hold_env.env_step_hold(env, hold_left, k, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den, hold_samples);
        const e: i64 = env.*;
        // invariant: the envelope stays a legal magnitude — never negative, never past the rail (the held step's own guarantee).
        assert(e >= 0 and e <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        if (e > thr) {
            const excess: i64 = e - thr; // strictly positive: the held KEY detector has crossed the ceiling
            // The compressed envelope target — ALES50's softened ceiling, computed on the KEY ENVELOPE not the target sample.
            const comp: i64 = thr + @divTrunc(excess * den, num);
            // invariant: the compressed envelope sits within [threshold, key envelope] — the gain comp/e is in (0, 1].
            assert(comp >= thr and comp <= e);
            const m: i64 = if (x < 0) -@as(i64, x) else @as(i64, x); // |x| — safe in i64 (|sample_min| = 32768 fits)
            // Apply the gain comp/e to the raw TARGET sample. m·comp <= 32768·32767 fits i64; divTrunc keeps it at most m.
            const new_m: i64 = @divTrunc(m * comp, e);
            // invariant: the gain reduces, never boosts — a keyed compressor only ever leans down.
            assert(new_m <= m);
            const y: i16 = @intCast(if (x < 0) -new_m else new_m); // safe: 0 <= new_m <= m <= 32768 fits i16 by sign
            target.buf[start + i] = y;
            // invariant: the written sample holds the target input's sign (magnitude only shrank).
            assert((x >= 0 and y >= 0) or (x <= 0 and y <= 0));
        }
        // a target sample whose held KEY envelope sits within the threshold is left exactly as it was — the gain reduction has not
        // opened; the key's level, not the target's, is what decides. A quiet target under a loud key still ducks; a loud target under
        // a silent key passes whole. That is the sidechain.
    }
    // invariant: a compressor changes target sample values only, never the target's length or bound, and never the key.
    assert(start + count <= target.len);
    assert(target.len <= target.buf.len);
}

/// Compress `count` samples starting at `start` in place in `target`, the gain reduction following a HELD envelope driven by `key` that
/// begins FROM SILENCE (a fresh env of 0 with the hold unarmed) — one implementation shared with the carried form, so the two cannot
/// drift. Refuses BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadCoeff, BadHold, BadRange, and BadKey before any write.
pub fn compress_key_follow(
    target: *timeline.Clip,
    key: *const timeline.Clip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    threshold: i32,
    ratio_num: u32,
    ratio_den: u32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
    hold_samples: u32,
) SidechainError!void {
    var env: i64 = 0;
    var hold_left: u32 = 0;
    return compress_key_follow_carry(target, key, start, count, &env, &hold_left, threshold, ratio_num, ratio_den, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den, hold_samples);
}

// --- selftest: silence stays silence; a SELF-KEY (the key holding the target's own samples) reduces to ALES58's attack/hold/release
//     compressor byte-for-byte (the grounding law — the sidechain generalizes the in-line compressor); a SILENT KEY never reduces (the
//     target passes whole however loud — the gain follows the key, not the signal); a LOUD KEY DUCKS a quiet target (a target below the
//     threshold is still turned down — ducking); UNIT RATIO is the identity everywhere; a loud target has its magnitude reduced with
//     the sign held and never expanding; the state CARRIES — env and hold_left both — so a span in two pieces equals the whole once;
//     and a bad threshold, a sub-unity ratio, an illegal coefficient, a hold past max_clip, an out-of-range span, and a short key each
//     refuse by name ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn abs16(v: i16) i32 {
    return if (v < 0) -@as(i32, v) else @as(i32, v);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. silence stays silence — an all-zero target compresses to all zeros under any key at any legal knobs.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&target, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&key, &[_]i16{ 9000, -9000, 9000, -9000, 9000, -9000 }); // loud key, silent target
        try compress_key_follow(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 3);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;
    }

    // 2. A SELF-KEY IS THE ORDINARY ALES58 COMPRESSOR — when the key holds the same samples as the target and is read unmutated, the
    //    keyed detector reads exactly what ALES58's in-line detector reads (ALES58 reads each x BEFORE it writes y, so an identical key
    //    reproduces its detector). The sidechain GENERALIZES the proven in-line compressor rather than replacing it. Proven by running
    //    BOTH tools on the same signal and demanding equality — the grounding law.
    {
        const loud = [_]i16{ 5000, -5000, 800, -9000, 9000, -200, 32767, -32767, 1200 };
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&target, &loud);
        var key = timeline.Clip{}; // the key holds the target's own samples — a self-key
        try load_clip(&key, &loud);
        try compress_key_follow(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5, 2);
        var inline_clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&inline_clip, &loud);
        try compress_env_hold.compress_hold_follow(&inline_clip, 0, inline_clip.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5, 2); // proven ALES58
        if (!eq(target.samples(), inline_clip.samples())) return error.SelfKeyNotInline;
    }

    // 3. A SILENT KEY NEVER REDUCES — with a silent key the held envelope never crosses the threshold, so the gain reduction never
    //    opens and the target passes byte-for-byte however loud it is. The gain follows the KEY, not the signal — the defining
    //    sidechain fact, stated positively.
    {
        const loud = [_]i16{ 20000, -20000, 32767, -32767, 15000, -15000 };
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&target, &loud);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&key, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 }); // a silent key
        try compress_key_follow(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0);
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &loud)) return error.SilentKeyReduced;
    }

    // 4. A LOUD KEY DUCKS A QUIET TARGET — a target sitting BELOW the threshold is still attenuated when the key is loud above it. An
    //    in-line compressor would leave that quiet target alone (its own envelope never crosses the threshold); the sidechain turns it
    //    down. This is DUCKING, proven. With instant attack the loud key seats its envelope above the threshold at once.
    {
        const quiet = [_]i16{ 400, -400, 400, -400, 400, -400 }; // a target wholly below the threshold
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&target, &quiet);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&key, &[_]i16{ 20000, 20000, 20000, 20000, 20000, 20000 }); // a loud key above the threshold
        try compress_key_follow(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1000, 8, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0); // instant attack, 8:1 ratio
        const out = target.samples();
        // an in-line compressor leaves `quiet` untouched (its own envelope, ~400, never crosses 1000); the ducker turns it down.
        var reduced_somewhere = false;
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < out.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(out[i]) > abs16(quiet[i])) return error.DuckExpanded; // never boosts
            if (abs16(out[i]) < abs16(quiet[i])) reduced_somewhere = true; // the loud key ducked the quiet target
        }
        if (!reduced_somewhere) return error.LoudKeyDidNotDuck;
        // prove it against the in-line compressor explicitly: keyed on itself, this quiet target would pass whole.
        var inline_clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&inline_clip, &quiet);
        try compress_env_hold.compress_hold_follow(&inline_clip, 0, inline_clip.len, 1000, 8, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0);
        if (!eq(inline_clip.samples(), &quiet)) return error.InlineShouldNotDuck; // sanity: in-line leaves the quiet target alone
    }

    // 5. UNIT RATIO IS THE IDENTITY EVERYWHERE — ratio_num == ratio_den makes the compressed envelope equal the key envelope, so the
    //    gain is exactly one at every sample regardless of the key, smoothing, or hold: the whole target passes byte-for-byte.
    {
        const loud = [_]i16{ 3000, -3000, 500, -20000, 20000, -400 };
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&target, &loud);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&key, &[_]i16{ 30000, 30000, 30000, 30000, 30000, 30000 }); // a loud key — irrelevant at unit ratio
        try compress_key_follow(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1000, 3, 3, 1, 4, 1, 8, 4); // unit ratio 3:3
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &loud)) return error.UnitRatioNotIdentity;
    }

    // 6. THE SIGN IS HELD AND THE MAGNITUDE NEVER EXPANDS — a loud target under a loud key compresses so every output magnitude is at
    //    most the target input's and every sign matches, under a real attack, release, and hold.
    {
        const loud = [_]i16{ 6000, -6000, 800, -12000, 12000, -300, 9000, -9000 };
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&target, &loud);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&key, &[_]i16{ 10000, -10000, 10000, -10000, 10000, -10000, 10000, -10000 });
        try compress_key_follow(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 2, 1, 6, 2);
        const out = target.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < out.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(out[i]) > abs16(loud[i])) return error.MagnitudeExpanded; // the gain only ever reduces
            if ((loud[i] > 0 and out[i] < 0) or (loud[i] < 0 and out[i] > 0)) return error.SignFlipped;
        }
    }

    // 7. THE STATE CARRIES — env AND hold_left both, the KEY's detector — so compressing a span in two pieces, the split falling
    //    MID-HOLD, equals compressing the whole once, byte-for-byte: the seam invisible, so a keyed compressor can span and automate.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 2000, -2000, 3000, -3000, 4000, -4000, 5000, -5000 };
        const keysig = [_]i16{ 14000, 14000, 400, 400, 400, 9000, 6000, 6000 };
        var whole = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&whole, &sig);
        var kwhole = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&kwhole, &keysig);
        try compress_key_follow(&whole, &kwhole, 0, whole.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 5, 2);

        var split = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&split, &sig);
        var ksplit = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&ksplit, &keysig);
        var env: i64 = 0;
        var hold_left: u32 = 0;
        try compress_key_follow_carry(&split, &ksplit, 0, 3, &env, &hold_left, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 5, 2); // split at index 3 mid-hold
        try compress_key_follow_carry(&split, &ksplit, 3, split.len - 3, &env, &hold_left, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 5, 2); // continues both
        if (!eq(split.samples(), whole.samples())) return error.CarryNotEqual;
    }

    // 8a. a zero threshold refuses BadThreshold before any write, the target untouched.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&target, &three);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&key, &[_]i16{ 9000, 9000, 9000 });
        if (compress_key_follow(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 0, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.ZeroThresholdSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongZeroThreshold;
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedThresholdMutated;
    }

    // 8b. a sub-unity ratio (would expand) refuses BadRatio before any write.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&target, &three);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&key, &[_]i16{ 9000, 9000, 9000 });
        if (compress_key_follow(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1000, 1, 2, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.SubUnitySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRatio) return error.WrongSubUnity;
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedRatioMutated;
    }

    // 8c. an illegal attack coefficient (numerator past the denominator) refuses BadCoeff before any write.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&target, &three);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&key, &[_]i16{ 9000, 9000, 9000 });
        if (compress_key_follow(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1000, 4, 1, 3, 2, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.BadAttackSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCoeff) return error.WrongBadAttack;
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedCoeffMutated;
    }

    // 8d. a hold longer than max_clip (no clip is that long) refuses BadHold before any write.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&target, &three);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&key, &[_]i16{ 9000, 9000, 9000 });
        if (compress_key_follow(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, timeline.max_clip + 1)) |_| return error.BadHoldSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadHold) return error.WrongBadHold;
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedHoldMutated;
    }

    // 8e. an out-of-range span (past the TARGET) refuses BadRange before any write, leaving the target untouched.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&target, &three);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&key, &[_]i16{ 9000, 9000, 9000, 9000, 9000 }); // a long-enough key isolates the TARGET range fault
        if (compress_key_follow(&target, &key, 2, 5, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedRangeMutated;
    }

    // 8f. a KEY shorter than the span refuses BadKey before any write — the detector would read past the key's samples. The target
    //     range is legal here (target as long as the span), so only the short key can be the fault.
    {
        var target = timeline.Clip{};
        const four = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4 };
        try load_clip(&target, &four);
        var key = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&key, &[_]i16{ 9000, 9000 }); // a key of only two samples under a span of four
        if (compress_key_follow(&target, &key, 0, target.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.ShortKeySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadKey) return error.WrongShortKey;
        if (!eq(target.samples(), &four)) return error.RefusedKeyMutated;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-sidechain: the SIDECHAIN compressor drives ALES50's softened ceiling from ALES56's HELD follower advanced on a " ++
        "KEY clip rather than on the target — the detector listens to the key while the gain lands on the target. Silence stays " ++
        "silence; a SELF-KEY (the key holding the target's own samples) reduces to ALES58's attack/hold/release compressor " ++
        "BYTE-FOR-BYTE (the grounding law — the sidechain generalizes the in-line compressor, proven by running both tools); a SILENT " ++
        "KEY never reduces (the target passes whole however loud — the gain follows the key, not the signal); a LOUD KEY DUCKS a quiet " ++
        "target (a target below the threshold turned down where the in-line compressor leaves it alone — ducking); UNIT RATIO is the " ++
        "identity everywhere; a loud target's magnitude reduces with the sign held and never expanding; the state CARRIES — env and " ++
        "hold_left both — so a span in two pieces equals the whole once (the seam invisible, the key's detector surviving the " ++
        "boundary); and a zero threshold refuses BadThreshold, a sub-unity ratio BadRatio, an illegal coefficient BadCoeff, a hold " ++
        "past max_clip BadHold, an out-of-range span BadRange, a short key BadKey, each leaving the target untouched; no socket, no " ++
        "network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
